---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BRAND } from '../lib/labels';
import { withBase, REPO_URL, REPO_ISSUES_URL } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${BRAND.name}: purpose, independence, coverage bias, and corrections.`}
>
  <div class="container-narrow pt-10 sm:pt-14 pb-16 prose-case">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-3">
      {BRAND.descriptor}
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl mb-4">About</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">
      {BRAND.name} is an independent public-record archive. It tracks
      high-profile criminal cases where Bitcoin was central, with citations you
      can open. {BRAND.promise}
    </p>

    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
      Public talk about “crypto crime” often mixes rumor, undated dollar
      figures, and guilt-by-association. This project prefers <strong
        >fewer cases, fully sourced</strong
      >, over a firehose of weak claims.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Neutral on the technology. Strict on the record.</strong> We
      neither cheerlead nor moralize Bitcoin; we document what courts and
      competent agencies put in the public domain.
    </p>

    <h2>Audience</h2>
    <p>
      Journalists, researchers, educators, and careful readers who want a
      starting point — not a substitute for reading the court file or agency
      release.
    </p>

    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>
      No affiliation with any government agency, exchange, law firm, or
      advocacy group. Content is editorial and incomplete by design. See
      <a href={withBase('/methodology/')}>methodology</a> for inclusion rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage bias (current archive)</h2>
    <p>
      Most primary sources today are <strong
        >U.S. Department of Justice and U.S. Attorney’s Office</strong
      > releases and related court materials. That is a real bias: foreign
      judgments, non-English records, and civil-only threads are underrepresented
      until we can cite them at the same standard. Absence of a case is not
      evidence that nothing happened.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If you find an error — wrong date, misstated amount, outdated legal
      status, or a missing primary source — please
      <a href={REPO_ISSUES_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue</a>
      or pull request on
      <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">the project repository</a>
      with a link to the primary record. Material corrections update the case’s
      <strong>last reviewed</strong> date and, when relevant, the timeline.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>We correct the record; we do not “balance” unsourced accusations.</li>
      <li>Appeals, sentence changes, and new seizures should be sourced the same way as original claims.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Prefer
      <a href={REPO_ISSUES_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Issues</a>
      for factual corrections (so the fix stays auditable). Source code:
      <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">{REPO_URL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Improvements are welcome when they improve accuracy: primary source links,
      clearer legal-status labels, careful new case files that meet the
      inclusion bar. Unsourced accusations will be rejected. Editorial rules for
      agents and humans live in <code>AGENTS.md</code> and
      <code>docs/BRAND.md</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
